WEBVTT

00:00:00.015 --> 00:00:02.676
Miss Brill, by Katherine Mansfield.

00:00:02.676 --> 00:00:15.902
Although it was so brilliantly fine—the blue sky powdered with gold and great spots of light like white wine splashed over the Jardins Publiques—Miss Brill was glad that she had decided on her fur.

00:00:15.902 --> 00:00:28.816
The air was motionless, but when you opened your mouth there was just a faint chill, like a chill from a glass of iced water before you sip, and now and again a leaf came drifting—from nowhere, from the sky.

00:00:28.816 --> 00:00:31.791
Miss Brill put up her hand and touched her fur.

00:00:31.791 --> 00:00:32.953
Dear little thing!

00:00:32.953 --> 00:00:34.984
It was nice to feel it again.

00:00:34.984 --> 00:00:44.511
She had taken it out of its box that afternoon, shaken out the moth-powder, given it a good brush, and rubbed the life back into the dim little eyes.

00:00:44.511 --> 00:00:48.651
“What has been happening to me?” said the sad little eyes.

00:00:48.651 --> 00:00:53.803
Oh, how sweet it was to see them snap at her again from the red eiderdown!...

00:00:53.803 --> 00:00:58.528
But the nose, which was of some black composition, wasn’t at all firm.

00:00:58.528 --> 00:01:00.939
It must have had a knock, somehow.

00:01:00.939 --> 00:01:08.350
Never mind—a little dab of black sealing-wax when the time came—when it was absolutely necessary....

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:09.420
Little rogue!

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:12.087
Yes, she really felt like that about it.

00:01:12.087 --> 00:01:15.637
Little rogue biting its tail just by her left ear.

00:01:15.637 --> 00:01:19.674
She could have taken it off and laid it on her lap and stroked it.

00:01:19.674 --> 00:01:25.263
She felt a tingling in her hands and arms, but that came from walking, she supposed.

00:01:25.263 --> 00:01:33.851
And when she breathed, something light and sad—no, not sad, exactly—something gentle seemed to move in her bosom.

00:01:33.851 --> 00:01:38.598
There were a number of people out this afternoon, far more than last Sunday.

00:01:38.598 --> 00:01:41.225
And the band sounded louder and gayer.

00:01:41.225 --> 00:01:43.711
That was because the Season had begun.

00:01:43.711 --> 00:01:49.587
For although the band played all the year round on Sundays, out of season it was never the same.

00:01:49.587 --> 00:01:56.714
It was like some one playing with only the family to listen; it didn’t care how it played if there weren’t any strangers present.

00:01:56.714 --> 00:01:59.564
Wasn’t the conductor wearing a new coat, too?

00:01:59.564 --> 00:02:01.360
She was sure it was new.

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:11.103
He scraped with his foot and flapped his arms like a rooster about to crow, and the bandsmen sitting in the green rotunda blew out their cheeks and glared at the music.

00:02:11.103 --> 00:02:17.288
Now there came a little “flutey” bit—very pretty!—a little chain of bright drops.

00:02:17.288 --> 00:02:19.381
She was sure it would be repeated.

00:02:19.381 --> 00:02:22.532
It was; she lifted her head and smiled.

00:02:22.532 --> 00:02:37.682
Only two people shared her “special” seat: a fine old man in a velvet coat, his hands clasped over a huge carved walking-stick, and a big old woman, sitting upright, with a roll of knitting on her embroidered apron.

00:02:37.682 --> 00:02:38.998
They did not speak.

00:02:38.998 --> 00:02:44.242
This was disappointing, for Miss Brill always looked forward to the conversation.

00:02:44.242 --> 00:02:53.790
She had become really quite expert, she thought, at listening as though she didn’t listen, at sitting in other people’s lives just for a minute while they talked round her.

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:56.903
She glanced, sideways, at the old couple.

00:02:56.903 --> 00:02:58.769
Perhaps they would go soon.

00:02:58.769 --> 00:03:02.561
Last Sunday, too, hadn’t been as interesting as usual.

00:03:02.561 --> 00:03:08.235
An Englishman and his wife, he wearing a dreadful Panama hat and she button boots.

00:03:08.235 --> 00:03:18.853
And she’d gone on the whole time about how she ought to wear spectacles; she knew she needed them; but that it was no good getting any; they’d be sure to break and they’d never keep on.

00:03:18.853 --> 00:03:20.716
And he’d been so patient.

00:03:20.716 --> 00:03:28.441
He’d suggested everything—gold rims, the kind that curved round your ears, little pads inside the bridge.

00:03:28.441 --> 00:03:30.606
No, nothing would please her.

00:03:30.606 --> 00:03:33.238
“They’ll always be sliding down my nose!”

00:03:33.238 --> 00:03:35.511
Miss Brill had wanted to shake her.

00:03:36.126 --> 00:03:39.698
The old people sat on the bench, still as statues.

00:03:39.698 --> 00:03:42.902
Never mind, there was always the crowd to watch.

00:03:42.902 --> 00:03:56.113
To and fro, in front of the flower-beds and the band rotunda, the couples and groups paraded, stopped to talk, to greet, to buy a handful of flowers from the old beggar who had his tray fixed to the railings.

00:03:56.113 --> 00:04:08.623
Little children ran among them, swooping and laughing; little boys with big white silk bows under their chins, little girls, little French dolls, dressed up in velvet and lace.

00:04:08.623 --> 00:04:24.111
And sometimes a tiny staggerer came suddenly rocking into the open from under the trees, stopped, stared, as suddenly sat down “flop,” until its small high-stepping mother, like a young hen, rushed scolding to its rescue.

00:04:24.111 --> 00:04:36.446
Other people sat on the benches and green chairs, but they were nearly always the same, Sunday after Sunday, and—Miss Brill had often noticed—there was something funny about nearly all of them.

00:04:36.446 --> 00:04:45.847
They were odd, silent, nearly all old, and from the way they stared they looked as though they’d just come from dark little rooms or even—even cupboards!

00:04:45.847 --> 00:04:55.643
Behind the rotunda the slender trees with yellow leaves down drooping, and through them just a line of sea, and beyond the blue sky with gold-veined clouds.

00:04:55.643 --> 00:05:02.623
Tum-tum-tum tiddle-um! tiddle-um! tum tiddley-um tum ta! blew the band.

00:05:02.623 --> 00:05:10.415
Two young girls in red came by and two young soldiers in blue met them, and they laughed and paired and went off arm-in-arm.

00:05:10.415 --> 00:05:17.555
Two peasant women with funny straw hats passed, gravely, leading beautiful smoke-coloured donkeys.

00:05:17.555 --> 00:05:20.262
A cold, pale nun hurried by.

00:05:20.262 --> 00:05:30.419
A beautiful woman came along and dropped her bunch of violets, and a little boy ran after to hand them to her, and she took them and threw them away as if they’d been poisoned.

00:05:30.419 --> 00:05:31.246
Dear me!

00:05:31.246 --> 00:05:34.500
Miss Brill didn’t know whether to admire that or not!

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:39.595
And now an ermine toque and a gentleman in grey met just in front of her.

00:05:39.595 --> 00:05:46.386
He was tall, stiff, dignified, and she was wearing the ermine toque she’d bought when her hair was yellow.

00:05:46.386 --> 00:05:59.434
Now everything, her hair, her face, even her eyes, was the same colour as the shabby ermine, and her hand, in its cleaned glove, lifted to dab her lips, was a tiny yellowish paw.

00:05:59.434 --> 00:06:02.704
Oh, she was so pleased to see him—delighted!

00:06:02.704 --> 00:06:05.579
She rather thought they were going to meet that afternoon.

00:06:05.579 --> 00:06:11.091
She described where she’d been—everywhere, here, there, along by the sea.

00:06:11.091 --> 00:06:13.765
The day was so charming—didn’t he agree?

00:06:13.765 --> 00:06:15.615
And wouldn’t he, perhaps?...

00:06:15.615 --> 00:06:26.876
But he shook his head, lighted a cigarette, slowly breathed a great deep puff into her face, and even while she was still talking and laughing, flicked the match away and walked on.

00:06:26.876 --> 00:06:31.669
The ermine toque was alone; she smiled more brightly than ever.

00:06:31.669 --> 00:06:39.080
But even the band seemed to know what she was feeling and played more softly, played tenderly, and the drum beat, “The Brute!

00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:41.270
The Brute!” over and over.

00:06:41.270 --> 00:06:42.412
What would she do?

00:06:42.412 --> 00:06:44.126
What was going to happen now?

00:06:44.126 --> 00:06:54.051
But as Miss Brill wondered, the ermine toque turned, raised her hand as though she’d seen some one else, much nicer, just over there, and pattered away.

00:06:54.051 --> 00:07:10.785
And the band changed again and played more quickly, more gayly than ever, and the old couple on Miss Brill’s seat got up and marched away, and such a funny old man with long whiskers hobbled along in time to the music and was nearly knocked over by four girls walking abreast.

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:13.937
Oh, how fascinating it was!

00:07:13.937 --> 00:07:15.185
How she enjoyed it!

00:07:15.185 --> 00:07:18.030
How she loved sitting here, watching it all!

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:19.546
It was like a play.

00:07:19.546 --> 00:07:21.607
It was exactly like a play.

00:07:21.607 --> 00:07:24.606
Who could believe the sky at the back wasn’t painted?

00:07:24.606 --> 00:07:38.426
But it wasn’t till a little brown dog trotted on solemn and then slowly trotted off, like a little “theatre” dog, a little dog that had been drugged, that Miss Brill discovered what it was that made it so exciting.

00:07:38.426 --> 00:07:40.199
They were all on the stage.

00:07:40.199 --> 00:07:44.482
They weren’t only the audience, not only looking on; they were acting.

00:07:44.482 --> 00:07:47.392
Even she had a part and came every Sunday.

00:07:47.392 --> 00:07:53.559
No doubt somebody would have noticed if she hadn’t been there; she was part of the performance after all.

00:07:53.559 --> 00:07:56.858
How strange she’d never thought of it like that before!

00:07:56.858 --> 00:08:13.297
And yet it explained why she made such a point of starting from home at just the same time each week—so as not to be late for the performance—and it also explained why she had quite a queer, shy feeling at telling her English pupils how she spent her Sunday afternoons.

00:08:13.297 --> 00:08:14.349
No wonder!

00:08:14.349 --> 00:08:16.928
Miss Brill nearly laughed out loud.

00:08:16.928 --> 00:08:18.555
She was on the stage.

00:08:18.555 --> 00:08:25.953
She thought of the old invalid gentleman to whom she read the newspaper four afternoons a week while he slept in the garden.

00:08:25.953 --> 00:08:33.818
She had got quite used to the frail head on the cotton pillow, the hollowed eyes, the open mouth and the high pinched nose.

00:08:33.818 --> 00:08:38.728
If he’d been dead she mightn’t have noticed for weeks; she wouldn’t have minded.

00:08:38.728 --> 00:08:43.324
But suddenly he knew he was having the paper read to him by an actress!

00:08:43.324 --> 00:08:44.397
“An actress!”

00:08:44.397 --> 00:08:49.380
The old head lifted; two points of light quivered in the old eyes.

00:08:49.380 --> 00:08:51.094
“An actress—are ye?”

00:08:51.094 --> 00:09:00.091
And Miss Brill smoothed the newspaper as though it were the manuscript of her part and said gently; “Yes, I have been an actress for a long time.”

00:09:00.091 --> 00:09:02.230
The band had been having a rest.

00:09:02.230 --> 00:09:03.784
Now they started again.

00:09:03.784 --> 00:09:15.573
And what they played was warm, sunny, yet there was just a faint chill—a something, what was it?—not sadness—no, not sadness—a something that made you want to sing.

00:09:15.573 --> 00:09:24.886
The tune lifted, lifted, the light shone; and it seemed to Miss Brill that in another moment all of them, all the whole company, would begin singing.

00:09:24.886 --> 00:09:33.473
The young ones, the laughing ones who were moving together, they would begin, and the men’s voices, very resolute and brave, would join them.

00:09:33.473 --> 00:09:45.559
And then she too, she too, and the others on the benches—they would come in with a kind of accompaniment—something low, that scarcely rose or fell, something so beautiful—moving....

00:09:45.559 --> 00:09:51.651
And Miss Brill’s eyes filled with tears and she looked smiling at all the other members of the company.

00:09:51.651 --> 00:09:58.032
Yes, we understand, we understand, she thought—though what they understood she didn’t know.

00:09:58.032 --> 00:10:03.475
Just at that moment a boy and girl came and sat down where the old couple had been.

00:10:03.475 --> 00:10:06.446
They were beautifully dressed; they were in love.

00:10:06.446 --> 00:10:11.268
The hero and heroine, of course, just arrived from his father’s yacht.

00:10:11.268 --> 00:10:17.533
And still soundlessly singing, still with that trembling smile, Miss Brill prepared to listen.

00:10:17.533 --> 00:10:20.066
“No, not now,” said the girl.

00:10:20.066 --> 00:10:21.690
“Not here, I can’t.”

00:10:21.690 --> 00:10:22.593
“But why?

00:10:22.593 --> 00:10:27.063
Because of that stupid old thing at the end there?” asked the boy.

00:10:27.063 --> 00:10:29.889
“Why does she come here at all—who wants her?

00:10:29.889 --> 00:10:32.769
Why doesn’t she keep her silly old mug at home?”

00:10:32.769 --> 00:10:36.600
“It’s her fu-fur which is so funny,” giggled the girl.

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:38.956
“It’s exactly like a fried whiting.”

00:10:39.555 --> 00:10:43.978
“Ah, be off with you!” said the boy in an angry whisper.

00:10:43.978 --> 00:10:47.153
Then: “Tell me, ma petite chère—”

00:10:47.153 --> 00:10:49.583
“No, not here,” said the girl.

00:10:49.583 --> 00:10:50.441
“Not yet.”

00:10:50.441 --> 00:10:55.237
On her way home she usually bought a slice of honey-cake at the baker’s.

00:10:55.237 --> 00:10:57.167
It was her Sunday treat.

00:10:57.167 --> 00:11:01.167
Sometimes there was an almond in her slice, sometimes not.

00:11:01.167 --> 00:11:03.089
It made a great difference.

00:11:03.089 --> 00:11:11.275
If there was an almond it was like carrying home a tiny present—a surprise—something that might very well not have been there.

00:11:11.275 --> 00:11:17.144
She hurried on the almond Sundays and struck the match for the kettle in quite a dashing way.

00:11:17.144 --> 00:11:27.078
But to-day she passed the baker’s by, climbed the stairs, went into the little dark room—her room like a cupboard—and sat down on the red eiderdown.

00:11:27.078 --> 00:11:29.283
She sat there for a long time.

00:11:29.283 --> 00:11:32.538
The box that the fur came out of was on the bed.

00:11:32.538 --> 00:11:38.105
She unclasped the necklet quickly; quickly, without looking, laid it inside.

00:11:38.105 --> 00:11:41.920
But when she put the lid on she thought she heard something crying.
